.TH OOB-USB-SERIAL 1 "2026-08-31" "oob-usb-serial 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
oob-usb-serial \- out-of-band USB serial console multiplexer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B oob-usb-serial
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B oob-usb-serial
discovers every USB-to-serial adapter attached to the host and opens each one
as a named window inside a single detached GNU
.BR screen (1)
session. Adapters are matched by their stable udev
.B ID_SERIAL
identifier rather than by their
.I /dev/ttyUSBx
node, which is not stable across reboots or replugs.
.PP
It is intended to turn a small Linux host, such as a Raspberry Pi Zero with a
USB hub and several serial adapters, into an out-of-band access box reachable
over SSH.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B discover
List discovered adapters as raw tab-separated records
(device node, ID_SERIAL, vendor, model).
.TP
.B list
Human-friendly table of adapters and their resolved configuration.
.TP
.B start
Generate the screenrc and start the detached session, one window per adapter.
.TP
.B attach
Attach to the session, starting it first if it is not running.
.TP
.B stop
Terminate the session.
.TP
.B status
Report whether the session is running.
.TP
.B interactive
Use
.BR dialog (1)
menus to choose a baud rate, a framing and a device, then open it. Framing is
either a preset (read from
.IR /usr/share/oob-usb-serial/serial_framings.txt )
or
.BR Custom ,
which prompts for data bits, parity, stop bits and flow control individually.
This opens a plain
.BR screen (1)
session; leave it with
.B Ctrl-a k
(kill the window) or
.B Ctrl-a \e
(quit screen).
.TP
.B config
Interactively map each discovered adapter into
.IR devices.conf ,
prompting for a window name, baud rate and framing (adapters already mapped are
skipped). Writing usually requires root.
.TP
.B config \-\-generate
Print the generated screenrc to standard output without making any changes.
.TP
.B help
Show usage information.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/oob-usb-serial/oob-usb-serial.conf
Global defaults: default baud rate, framing, session name and OOB user.
.TP
.I /etc/oob-usb-serial/devices.conf
Per-device mappings in INI format. Each
.RI [ section ]
names a screen window and contains a
.B match
glob (against udev ID_SERIAL) plus optional
.B baud
and
.B framing
keys.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/oob-usb-serial
Bash tab-completion for the subcommands.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR screen (1),
.BR stty (1),
.BR udevadm (8)
.SH AUTHOR
diaznet
